139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186088151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311051 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186088151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311530 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186088151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186088151 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\nx\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186088151 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 4x^{2}, y(1) = 2$\nSelect the correct options below. An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186088151 1074 "The first step is to write the equation in standard form,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot 4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 4x \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={1}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311682 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311696 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311697 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311699 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311700 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311719 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311720 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311930 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311931 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311933 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311934 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311941 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311942 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186092177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311949 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186096653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311617 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186096653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311618 13194139533416 17592186045585 tru