n$$\n3=30I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+30I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+30{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-30t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+30\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-30t}+\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-30(0)}+\\frac{1}{10} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{9}{10}{e}^{-30t}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(9{e}^{-30t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{9}{10}{e}^{-30t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{10}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117708 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186120264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311548 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120264 1071 "![](images/9f/9f7c87d6914b44c303fa7c67c9f5bb4908cf3e30912886d5945eddb899549032.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120264 1071 "![](images/9f/9f7c87d6914b44c303fa7c67c9f5bb4908cf3e30912886d5945eddb899549032.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.The amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186120264 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=9I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+9{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(9\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +9\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 9\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{15})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}}≈0.84\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117783 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186120279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311438 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120279 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = \\frac{π}{3}$, given that\n$r(t)|= ⟨\\cos 3t, 4t,-\\sin 3t⟩; T(t)|= \\frac{1}{5}⟨ - 3\\sin 3t, 4,-3\\cos 3t⟩; N(t)|= ⟨ - \\cos 3t, 0, \\sin 3t⟩$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186120279 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,4t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120279 1074 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩| \\\\  & =|\\,0+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}+0\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117795 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186120281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311414 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120281 1071 "Find the arc length of the curve $f(t) = ⟨\\cos t + t\\sin t, \\sin t - t\\cos t, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}t^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t = 1$ to $t = 3$." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186120281 1071 "Find the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186120281 1074 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 131941