m the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ (-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144342 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186147779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144347 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186147779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310838 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310849 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311176 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311390 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147779 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = x^{3}e^{5x}$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_{1}(x) = e^{x}$ and $y_{2} = e^{2x}$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$u_{1}^{′}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(e^{2x})^{′}$ {{field-1}}\nwhere $u_{1} = u_{1}(x), u_{2} = u_{2}(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n$u_{1}^{′}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(e^{2x})^{′}$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186147779 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147779 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}} & =\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144362 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186147781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311364 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147781 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = x^{3}e^{5x}$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_{1}(x) = e^{x}$ and $y_{2} = e^{2x}$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution\n$y_{p}(x) = u_{1}(x)y_{1} + u_{2}(x)y_{2}$\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n${u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = x^{3}e^{5x}; u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0$\n\nGiven that $u_{1}(x) =-\\intg(x)dx$, find the function $g$." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186147781 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186147781 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311912 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311913 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311914 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311918 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311916 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311917 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311922 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311909 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311911 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311915 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311920 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149898 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186152578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149901 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186152578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149904 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186152578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311192 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311378 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311549 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186152578 1071 "Determine which of the following second-order O