8 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311555 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311556 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311557 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311558 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311560 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311561 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311562 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311563 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311564 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228267 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310924 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229686 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229686 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n1. $F_{1}(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}t^{2}x\\cos (tx)dx, t ∈ R$\n2. $F_{2}(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1)/((x - t)^{2} + 1)dx, t ∈ R$\n3. $F_{3}(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x - t}dx, t < 0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229686 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228279 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311261 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229688 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229688 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-3y(t),\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} & te^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229688 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228332 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311066 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229696 1071 "![](images/3a/3a03159ef7285c7cbb571d8870edce98452b197d178334b4b06277f05a38d876.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{125}C$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229696 1071 "![](images/3a/3a03159ef7285c7cbb571d8870edce98452b197d178334b4b06277f05a38d876.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229696 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{50}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{9}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{125},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A{e}^{-50(0)/3}+\\frac{9}{500} & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(9-5{e}^{-50t/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{9}{500}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228357 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311015 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229701 1071 "The charge $q(t)$, in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dq}{dt} + 6q = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229701 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+6q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229701 1074 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+6{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{1}{12}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{1}{12}$ coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228358 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311473 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229702 1071 "![](images/49/497263348010d38f2372e818b89ee2294d8f207366521f2bddce4a0628f457b1.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229702 1071 "![](images/49/497263348010d38f2372e818b89ee2294d8f207366521f2bddce4a0628f457b1.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$q(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\frac{P}{145}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{Q}{29}\\sin (2t)$\nwhere $A$, $P$, and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P + Q$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229702 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{145},\\beta =\\frac{1}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-2,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-2+1=-1.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228364 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228368 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228373 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311016 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311270 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311505 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229703 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix $Φ_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n$Φ_{0}^{′}(t) = AΦ_{0}(t), Φ_{0}(0) = I_{n}$.\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(t_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $Φ_{0}(t) = e^{At}$\n> $L2$: $x(t) = Φ_{0}(t)c$ for some constant vector $c$\n> $L3$: $x(t) = e^{At}c$\n> $L4$: $x(t_{0}) = e^{At_{0}}c$\n> $L5$: $(e^{At_{0}})^{-1}x(t_{0}) = (e^{At_{0}})^{-1}e^{At_{0}}c$\n> $L6$: $c = e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0})$\n> $L7$: $x(t) = e^{At}e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0})$\n> $L8$: $x(t) = e^{At - At_{0}}x(t_{0})$\n> $L9$: $x(t) = e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0})$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ is true by {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from $L3$ {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L8$ follows from $L7$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229703 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ is true by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229703 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution of the system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ is true by $\\text{the properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Starting from $\\text{L3},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe evaluate both sides at $t={t}_{0}$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ $\\text{by substituting }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228385 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311365 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229706 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229706 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$y = x(1 + \\frac{dy}{dx}) + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}$.\n\nIf $y^{′}(x) = t$, then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t))$, where\n\n$x(t) = Ce^{-t} + f(t)$\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + x =-2t$.\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n$\\intte^{t}dt = (t - 1)e^{t}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229706 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =-2t{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =-2t{e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =-2(t-1)+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+2-2t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=2-2t.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228391 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311042 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229707 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(1-t)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229707 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$y = x(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dx})$.\n\nIf $y^{′}(x) = t$, then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t))$, where\n$y(t) = ((\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t + C)/((1 - t)^{2}))f(t) + g(t)$\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t) + g(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{2x}{t - 1} = (1)/(t(1 - t))$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229707 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1}=(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(t-1)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-\\frac{t-1}{t} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=\\frac{1-t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-t}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},g(t)=t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}+t.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228417 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311427 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229712 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229712 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n$y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 9y = \\frac{2e^{3x}}{1 + x^{2}}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x} + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229712 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})-(3{e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}+3x{e}^{6x}-3x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot x{e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}) \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to use substitution $t=1+{x}^{2}$ to find ${u}_{1}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))+(x{e}^{3x})(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+{{{e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228423 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311544 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229713 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)={y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186229713 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_{1}(y) = x\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n2. $D_{2}(y) = y^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n3. $D_{3}(y) = 2\\frac{d^{3}y}{dx^{3}} - x\\frac{dy}{dx} + y^{2}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 false] [17592186229713 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a functio