045494 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311549 1077 "field-3" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311549 1079 #edb/ref 17592186309443 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311549 1080 #uuid "ffbf3bc9-8beb-5ba5-93fe-be2939eaa8df" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310806 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311036 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311208 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311249 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311335 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311528 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1069 "q-335155" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x+y+{w}^{2},\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2y-z,\\, & z(0)=2 \\\\ {w}^{′}=-w+xz,\\, & w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(1)≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z(1)≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w(1)≈$\n{{field-6}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1072 #uuid "5dc89dde-1975-50ed-8fba-57a94707c276" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311550 1074 "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=2,$ and $w(0)=1,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=2,$ and $w=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y+{w}^{2} \\\\  & =0+1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2y-z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-w+xz \\\\  & =-1+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $0$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+0=2 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈3,$\n$z(1)≈2,$\nand\n$w(1)≈0.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311283 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311551 1069 "q-33517" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311551 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311551 1072 #uuid "a2edb181-6dbf-5979-80f4-12f00da0e0f3" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311551 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩ & =c\\,⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t-1}=c{e}^{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting $c=1$ into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$, and $t=3.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,{e}^{-1}⟩ \\\\ r(3) & =⟨{3}^{3},\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{3-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2}).$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311398 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311552 1069 "q-335207" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311552 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311552 1072 #uuid "7ec6cfc1-e844-58cb-9d89-165931802f08" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311552 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310953 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311553 1069 "q-335212" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311553 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311553 1072 #uuid "b412f2e8-8f4e-59d5-812a-2d162a6c359c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311553 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311122 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311554 1069 "q-335213" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311554 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+5y(t)-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311554 1072 #uuid "5f5893f7-3112-574c-a435-c387df346ccd" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311554 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310898 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311555 1069 "q-335246" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311555 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311555 1072 #uuid "0a8a1841-dc47-5a84-bd2d-e5c201943d9f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311555 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311374 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311556 1069 "q-335247" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311556 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311556 1072 #uuid "9de6d57f-11dc-5bb2-bebc-d38ef2773685" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311556 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,