o, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have \n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-(-2))}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310852 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311601 1069 "q-335454" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311601 1071 "Which statement correctly describes an **unstable** equilibrium point?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311601 1072 #uuid "4c4c0978-c7d9-5bf7-a6cc-7ff1c2d73636" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311601 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSome solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually leave a fixed neighborhood of it." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311064 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311602 1069 "q-335456" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311602 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(1<X<4).$\n\n$P(1<X<4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311602 1072 #uuid "a40ab8d7-d0b2-5cf8-b5f7-4b99b31f53fa" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311602 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<4) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4})+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311143 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [1