 close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Stable but not asymptotically stable.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310839 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310846 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311112 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311333 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311410 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1069 "q-335468" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1071 "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=O\\,({e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,f=O({e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(1)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines \n{{field-4}} and \n{{field-5}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1072 #uuid "d9fc7373-ebc0-5ca4-abd8-883fd57c8fd2" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311607 1074 "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=O\\,({e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L2}$ and $\\text{the absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})=O\\,({e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(1).\n$$\n\nThus,  by lines $\\text{L3 and L4}$ and $\\text{the transitive property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310855 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311608 1069 "q-335470" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311608 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311608 1072 #uuid "ca82624e-361e-5ab0-8f3d-deb7e2b73fe4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311608 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310888 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311133 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311140 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311356 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311490 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1069 "q-335474" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1071 "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5,$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{5})=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1072 #uuid "a6ca3d19-1c03-5a3f-9e6d-713b90d2c7f4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311609 1074 "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{4{x}^{2}-x+5}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $5$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\text{the constant multiple property}$ that\n\n$$\nf=O(1)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311226 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311610 1069 "q-335478" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311610 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[3,\\,0]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311610 1072 #uuid "f114f070-3aa1-5126-a2d1-72025fe298d9" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311610 1074 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+3,\\,y(t)=v(t),\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+3)^{2}-6(u(t)+3)+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+6u(t)+9)-6u(t)-18+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+3)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+12-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311080 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311611 1069 "q-335484" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311611 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\,3]^{T}.$ Two linearly independent eigenvectors of the system's matrix are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Bu.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvector of the matrix $B?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311611 1072 #uuid "b72c3d1f-8eb6-5972-9e5b-a9b662d68cdd" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311611 1074 "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $B$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvectors as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvectors are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2},$ or any nonzero vectors that are parallel to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only vector that is nonzero and is guaranteed to be parallel to either ${w}_{1}$ or ${w}_{2}$ is the vector $5{w}_{1}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310999 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311612 1069 "q-335485" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311612 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[-2,\\,4]^{T}.$ The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Au.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvalue of the matrix $A?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311612 1072 #uuid "3ddd0c95-9d32-5926-af86-97c6ac159ca5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311612 1074 "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $A$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvalues as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ or any expressions that are equal to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only quantity that is guaranteed to be an eigenvalue of $A$ is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311324 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311613 1069 "q-335488" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311613 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=-4x+1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311613 1072 #uuid "b8143e4f-528e-5eef-b81d-03c2d102e474" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311613 1074 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfies the above conditions is $y=-4x.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311382 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311614 1069 "q-335515" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311614 1071 "A system of ODEs has an unstable saddle point at ${x}^{∗}.$ The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following best describes the point $(0,0)$ for the shifted system?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311614 1072 #uuid "05ade684-7cd1-5147-822c-7acb8eafb6fe" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311614 1074 "Shifting does not alter the local behavior near an equilibrium. So, the equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.).\n\nRecall that the shift moves the equilibrium to the origin.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at $(0,0)$ in the shifted system has the same classification as ${x}^{∗}$ in the original system.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"An unstable saddle point\"." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311224 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311615 1069 "q-335516" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311615 1071 "A system of ODEs has a stable star node at ${x}^{∗}.$ The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following best describes the point $(0,0)$ for the shifted system?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311615 1072 #uuid "be705985-f360-5efb-b30b-33ee0bfc03e2" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311615 1074 "Shifting does not alter the local behavior near an equilibrium. So, the equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.).\n\nRecall that the shift moves the equilibrium to the origin.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at $(0,0)$ in the shifted system has the same classification as ${x}^{∗}$ in the original system.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"A stable star node\"." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310942 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311616 1069 "q-335522" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311616 1071 "A nonlinear system of ODEs has exactly two equilibrium points. The system is then shifted using the transformation $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ where ${x}^{∗}$ is one of the equilibria. Which of the following statements about the number of equilibria in the shifted system is guaranteed to be true?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311616 1072 #uuid "a9156c54-a0f8-59e7-b01b-764ac89d4134" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311616 1074 "A shift of coordinates is a translation of the phase space and does not alter the structure of the system’s equilibrium set. Each equilibrium point of the original system is translated to a corresponding equilibrium in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, although one of the equilibria is moved to the origin, the total number of equilibrium points remains the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311538 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311617 1069 "q-335529" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311617 1071 "![](images/6e/6e5d4cef21454855d29639d236ead4c2ad6cfcb66d2b8adaa7df16405f6d250c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{TR}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{MP}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MT}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QR}$ in terms of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{QR}=$   \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311617 1072 #uuid "e4f4d902-f700-5676-a087-0cef5fc46f7a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311617 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $R$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QR}=\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c42a0b8323c282a573244591d900801194f2f5b7382aabe60dedd903b896a029.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QP}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PM}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{MT}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TR}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/98/9876c3c4947aa9d61e687138bfb6fb26040e7c334af8b02914f5857ae1616e98.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QR} & =\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR} \\\\  & =b-c+d+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311296 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311618 1069 "q-335530" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311618 1071 "![](images/2a/2a4aa0aea855c84afb701ed5746dd8aa64c7644990a858a3f554014880b5657c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{PT}=b,$ and  $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ in terms of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ and $c.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=$   \n {{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311618 1072 #uuid "6b77d60c-7e14-524f-852d-94f25f149c38" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311618 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $P$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\overset{\\to }{PT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6bfe5ad7a1695d5208504f1666014737b020976d73419f4be273b5a84097e242.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PT}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TR}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/86/86a8fb7563c4a8874fc6d9b460c8e93bcf2c96e1b4e63a4f56f49f7326d2dcca.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ} \\\\  & =b-c+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311108 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311619 1069 "q-335558" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311619 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311619 1072 #uuid "e8292691-120f-5184-8aec-a200787b2456" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311619 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ x(4x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get $y=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311039 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311620 1069 "q-335578" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311620 1071 "Throughout an experiment in a laboratory, a culture contains $1\\,200$ bacteria in total (limited by nutrients and space). At the beginning of the experiment, $200$ of the $1\\,200$ bacteria are resistant to a new antibiotic. After $4$ hours, the number of resistant bacteria increases to $500.$ To the nearest hour, how long does it take for the number of resistant bacteria to reach $900?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of resistant bacteria, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in hours.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311620 1072 #uuid "169c8d2a-baf7-5d16-8418-7c154f48a72f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311620 1074 "The rate at which the resistant bacteria population, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total bacteria population in the culture and its current resistant population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total bacteria population the culture can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,200$ bacteria, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,200-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,200-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,200-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,200-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial population of resistant bacteria was $200$ cells. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=200,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K{e}^{0} & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K & =200 \\\\ K & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $4$ hours, the resistant bacteria population was $500$ cells. So, applying the condition $P(4)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-4k} & =500 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,000{e}^{-4k} \\\\ {e}^{-4k} & =\\frac{700}{1\\,000}=\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $900$ bacteria are resistant to the antibiotic. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =900 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =900 \\\\ 300 & =1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{300}{1\\,000}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $900$ bacteria will be resistant to the antibiotic after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})}{-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})}=13.502…≈14\\,\\text{hours},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest hour." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310865 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311621 1069 "q-335646" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311621 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311621 1072 #uuid "01df671d-eb13-51a1-8aa6-931227001511" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311621 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310969 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311622 1069 "q-335647" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311622 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311622 1072 #uuid "db5a2e69-356d-5e6a-bf94-b1893ca52642" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311622 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2y-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2(-x-2)-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\text{ }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-2=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=-(-2)-2=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310821 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311623 1069 "q-335657" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311623 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{3}(t)+x(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311623 1072 #uuid "9eb2e7ac-d5ca-5678-9359-e4e2a4d80ebd" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311623 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+x-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+(1-y)-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $x:$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=1-0=1.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get $x=1-1=0.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=1-(-1)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310929 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311624 1069 "q-335684" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311624 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311624 1072 #uuid "e119bc87-3c36-5636-abc9-412c368c9545" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311624 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}} & =0 \\\\ 8t-7 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311375 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311625 1069 "q-335687" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311625 1071 "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{2}-10t+3,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311625 1072 #uuid "3984878a-8ab1-5db4-bf0b-08ef4a7853a8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311625 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-10t+3) \\\\  & =4t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-10 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =10 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311053 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311626 1069 "q-335696" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311626 1071 "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311626 1072 #uuid "9d5a5da3-7219-5eb7-95de-5d3be4836fef" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311626 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-1 \\\\ t & ={e}^{-1} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{1}{e}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310997 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311164 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311627 1069 "q-335713" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311627 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=3t{e}^{-2t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311627 1072 #uuid "0750b1fe-1a76-56e4-9c4e-23cb062aa34d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311627 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+3t(-2){e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(3-6t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (3-6t){e}^{-2t} & <0 \\\\ 3-6t & <0 \\\\ -6t & <-3 \\\\ t & >\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311399 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311628 1069 "q-335797" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311628 1071 "![](images/57/57fd283dafc8ee7da736fd8b9dcccd22481d71fbf79533636ad6b2027776de37.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311628 1072 #uuid "9625e434-402c-5221-84ca-61c4f0b05d59" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311628 1074 "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-2$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-2,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,3).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/13/1373e779f491868cebc5b9954d055a7241e46d6f7a4ef7ba595b5c0687c270d3.png)" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311470 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311629 1069 "q-33581" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311629 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311629 1072 #uuid "8b1aa988-c2c7-5b7c-8814-04c3301ea2f6" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311629 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310917 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311256 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311630 1069 "q-335811" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311630 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2y}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},\\,y(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311630 1072 #uuid "eb60ed14-2886-5407-9edd-93c36aa77da2" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311630 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.$ \n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(\\pi )=0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+C}{{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311234 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311539 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311631 1069 "q-335812" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311631 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},\\,x>0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311631 1072 #uuid "3bbe03fd-0d2c-564c-9b93-a7997b3f7696" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311631 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yx & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2C}{\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310941 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311632 1069 "q-335824" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311632 1071 "![](images/96/96df34b3f844ee5abace182769011a3b4b2e413100fbca4cf25e829ca67948cc.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311632 1072 #uuid "e65967cd-c336-53ba-a105-1c4f34ff015c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311632 1074 "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2,$ $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4,6.$\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$ This does not correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4,6.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311173 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311633 1069 "q-335826" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311633 1071 "![](images/4a/4ad1c49955142936f8b6fef257af8021e4eb9aa576cd37ebed149e5a31fbedc8.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{EF}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{GE}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{AF}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GC}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ in terms of  $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{AC}=$   \n {{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311633 1072 #uuid "f781a193-08c4-51cc-baa2-0464ba327225" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311633 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $A$ to $C$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\overset{\\to }{AF}+\\overset{\\to }{FE}+\\overset{\\to }{EG}+\\overset{\\to }{GC}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e24ac0b6cf4f039cd1e8b2e1aa5dd921ea637fb2fe46666a0091372dfce87d44.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AF}=c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{FE}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{EG}=-b\\,\\overset{\\to }{GC}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/37/37c3758dc7a4c8a5b79c3e687e77f62090282410e3bf45bfade3494cd3b8055b.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\overset{\\to }{AF}+\\overset{\\to }{FE}+\\overset{\\to }{EG}+\\overset{\\to }{GC} \\\\  & =c+(-a)+(-b)+d \\\\  & =c-a-b+d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311031 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311634 1069 "q-335848" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311634 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(5,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 7.84).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 7.84)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311634 1072 #uuid "c2ed58bc-a791-5e8b-aac9-35b2a977d20a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311634 1074 "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 7.84) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{7.84-5}{4}) \\\\  & =P\\,(Z\\ge \\frac{2.84}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a5bf63797840ba4b457ef4f8564b979e106fa916b2247937733157830d6f443d.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.71) & =1-P(Z<0.71) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.71) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.71)=0.7611.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.71) & =1-0.7611 \\\\  & =0.2389.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 7.84)=0.2389.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311306 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311635 1069 "q-335886" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311635 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(-2,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge -4.48).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2743\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3085\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge -4.48)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311635 1072 #uuid "84bc16ec-d799-5442-844b-2ae98dc27092" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311635 1074 "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-2$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.48) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.48-(-2)}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -0.62).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/25/2547a2e847c503d8cdf6c2df258edd7c51e1478185f2cb0055f72425ed6fd04e.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.62) & =1-P(Z<-0.62) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.62) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.62).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.62)=0.2676.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.62) & =1-Φ(-0.62) \\\\  & =1-0.2676 \\\\  & =0.7324.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.48)=0.7324.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311166 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311636 1069 "q-335887" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311636 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(8,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 1.88).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0166\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0207\\,$ | $\\,0.0202\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 1.88)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311636 1072 #uuid "017068dc-43b7-5a74-9e0e-ac9e2605f2f7" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311636 1074 "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =8$ and $\\sigma =3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1.88) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1.88-8}{3}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -2.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c12f91dda20714f4b97a061a52d760249c0f9d9ce5029343af3e4c216981f43d.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.04) & =1-P(Z<-2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.04)=0.0207.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.04) & =1-Φ(-2.04) \\\\  & =1-0.0207 \\\\  & =0.9793.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1.88)=0.9793.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310885 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311170 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311637 1069 "q-335891" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311637 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311637 1072 #uuid "2b0c639d-6d20-531c-98aa-3a479abbd77e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311637 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-20}{2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(t-2)}{2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(t-2)}{\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ \\frac{5(t-2)}{\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}-20t+29}} & >0 \\\\ 5(t-2) & >0 \\\\ t-2 & >0 \\\\ t & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (2,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310826 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311316 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311638 1069 "q-335894" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311638 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311638 1072 #uuid "a588ded8-8fa7-577d-98ce-b0e2d62dd61d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311638 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6t-6}{2\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(t-1)}{2\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(t-1)}{\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{3(t-1)}{\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} & <0 \\\\ 3(t-1) & <0 \\\\ t-1 & <0 \\\\ t & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (0,1).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311134 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311285 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311639 1069 "q-335899" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311639 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2),$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311639 1072 #uuid "ced43c4a-1606-5ebf-b2c2-c00187d79874" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311639 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\sqrt{t}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}} & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & >0 \\\\ t & >{e}^{0} \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (1,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310972 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311245 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311640 1069 "q-335904" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311640 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by \n$s(t)=(4t-12)^{2},$\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311640 1072 #uuid "7817a831-30fb-5a8d-941e-c3b8c37a8268" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311640 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,((4t-12)^{2}) \\\\  & =8(4t-12) \\\\  & =32(t-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 32(t-3) & <0 \\\\ t-3 & <0 \\\\ t & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$ is\n\n$$\nt\\in [0,\\,3).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310876 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311071 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311641 1069 "q-335906" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311641 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{5}+5t,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311641 1072 #uuid "0652a3b6-cc06-5d64-844d-8bfe3dff9b13" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311641 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}+5t) \\\\  & =5{t}^{4}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{4}+5) \\\\  & =20{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=0.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =5(0)^{4}+5 \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=0,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=5.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311125 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311144 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311642 1069 "q-335909" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311642 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(4t-8){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311642 1072 #uuid "8aaec012-5a49-5605-a0fa-b3ea1f97ccff" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311642 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-8){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-8){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-4){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-4){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-4){e}^{t} \\\\  & =4t\\,{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t\\,{e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ 4t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=0.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(0)=(4\\cdot 0-4){e}^{0}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=0,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311186 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311268 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311643 1069 "q-335910" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311643 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(3t-6){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311643 1072 #uuid "3d295142-93a4-5b3b-aeca-50225771a54d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311643 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-6){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}+(3t-6)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(9-3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((9-3t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(-3){e}^{-t}+(9-3t)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(3t-12){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3t-12){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 3t-12 & =0 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=4.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(4)=(9-3\\cdot 4){e}^{-4}=-3{e}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=4,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-3{e}^{-4}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310930 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310931 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311644 1069 "q-335917" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311644 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}ln\\,({t}^{2}),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311644 1072 #uuid "21ef4a3a-3143-5d28-9b7b-5c36663b99e0" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311644 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}ln\\,({t}^{2})=2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(2t)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,({e}^{-3/2}) & ={e}^{-3/2}(4ln\\,({e}^{-3/2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(4\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(-6+2) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t={e}^{-3/2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4{e}^{-3/2}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311203 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311441 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311645 1069 "q-335924" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311645 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311645 1072 #uuid "fb520858-1659-5942-b7f5-935661020acc" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311645 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-1 \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311258 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311646 1069 "q-335957" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311646 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ (as shown in the table below) and the cumulative distribution function $F(y),$ compute $F(3).$\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $0.20$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ | $0.30$ |\n\n$F(3)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311646 1072 #uuid "0a1161e7-3e2c-5482-abe2-3d3bff291106" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311646 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =P(Y\\le 3) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {0,2}) \\\\  & =P(Y=0)+P(Y=2) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(2) \\\\  & =0.20+0.25 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311430 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311647 1069 "q-335964" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311647 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ (as shown in the table below) and the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$, compute $F(2)-F(1).$\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $0.343$ | $0.441$ | $0.189$ | $0.027$ |\n\n$F(2)-F(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311647 1072 #uuid "65ceebbb-e775-5cfd-9625-2f5c9d545eb8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311647 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nComputing $F(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & =P(Y\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =0.343+0.441 \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $F(2)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =P(Y\\le 2) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =0.343+0.441+0.189 \\\\  & =0.973.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nF(2)-F(1)=0.973-0.784=0.189\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310886 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311648 1069 "q-335991" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311648 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X=2).$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.10$ | $0.40$ | $0.70$ | $1.00$ |\n\n$P(X=2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311648 1072 #uuid "4648b894-9530-5019-b0ae-c73a1713759f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311648 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {0,1})+P(X=2) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =F(1)+P(X=2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =F(2)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.70-0.40 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311171 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311649 1069 "q-335992" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311649 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X=2).$\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $0.60$ | $0.85$ | $1.00$ |\n\n$P(X=2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311649 1072 #uuid "33cc58bc-7c52-5dff-983c-8da77e830d26" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311649 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {-1,0,1,2}) \\\\  & =P(X=-1)+P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {-1,0,1})+P(X=2) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =F(1)+P(X=2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =F(2)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.60-0.35 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311179 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311650 1069 "q-335993" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311650 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X=5).$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.20$ | $0.45$ | $0.70$ | $0.90$ | $1.00$ |\n\n$P(X=5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311650 1072 #uuid "bd541aa5-aa86-5ac7-98d7-1457c91d3245" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311650 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {2,3,4})+P(X=5) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =F(4)+P(X=5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =F(5)-F(4) \\\\  & =0.90-0.70 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311311 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311651 1069 "q-335999" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311651 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X=4).$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{7}{10}$ | $\\frac{9}{10}$ | $1$ |\n\n$P(X=4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311651 1072 #uuid "77866e05-ae39-560c-9933-314525311b2e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311651 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =P(X\\in {1,2,3})+P(X=4) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =F(3)+P(X=4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =F(4)-F(3) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311514 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311652 1069 "q-336006" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311652 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(1<X\\le 3).$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ | $\\frac{11}{20}$ | $\\frac{17}{20}$ | $1$ |\n\n$P(1<X\\le 3)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311652 1072 #uuid "9d68f343-9c56-55da-ae2d-7b64a968369a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311652 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $X$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 3) & =F(3)-F(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{20}-\\frac{7}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311211 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311653 1069 "q-336034" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311653 1071 "![](images/e6/e6a178edcc79e9e36553acf436209525b490dbefde06823d571f8e317dab0086.png)\n\nThe slope field of the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line of the given equation?" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311653 1072 #uuid "1d972b89-e88e-5bbe-8c44-44578e8b9c27" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311653 1074 "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y=-1$ and $y=1$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y\\in (1,\\infty ),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-1,1),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis.\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions.\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0.$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0.$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/62/62b3d23025e8b2716dd584678f1aef2ba3132a66250d0999cb13a2281347ee89.png)" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310919 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311055 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311320 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1069 "q-336093" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states the independence of the columns of {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ by the {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1072 #uuid "3d9725c1-fcc5-57a9-8b5a-bd5efbda8207" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311654 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L2}.$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states that the two columns of $Φ(t)$ are linearly independent, which is required of a fundamental matrix. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ states independence of the columns of $Φ(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the $\\text{properties of invertible matrices}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310799 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311317 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311445 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1069 "q-336110" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=4+3t \\\\ y=1-4t \\\\ z=-3-2t\\end{aligned}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,-4y\\,-\\,$ \n{{field-2}} $z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$\\,=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1072 #uuid "da280df0-7d7a-5d86-8e85-f52e5903aba5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311655 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,-2,5⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-4,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,-4,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ & =⟨1,-2,5⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,-2⟩ & =1 \\\\ 3x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 3x-4y-2z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311209 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311250 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311302 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1069 "q-336111" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=5+3t \\\\ z=7-t\\end{aligned}.$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1072 #uuid "8add1d49-d5c8-5e59-a059-69a179339ffb" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311656 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,4,1⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨2,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =⟨-3,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z & =5 \\\\ 2x+3y-z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311385 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311421 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311494 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1069 "q-336112" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1072 #uuid "556f46a4-05a0-5daf-9526-dc3855ec8f34" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311657 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,-4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,-4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =12 \\\\ x-4y+2z & =12 \\\\ x-4y+2z-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311197 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311658 1069 "q-336118" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311658 1071 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)={t}^{4}+{t}^{2}-5.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311658 1072 #uuid "8c6c8eeb-d01f-533e-bed4-2b8ea6d13d39" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311658 1074 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311413 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311659 1069 "q-336130" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311659 1071 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311659 1072 #uuid "123379b4-b283-5d57-933b-9e195e37586e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311659 1074 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t(-{csc}^{2}⁡t))}{{cot}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{csc}^{2}⁡t}{t})}{{cot}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{csc}^{2}⁡t}{t{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310944 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311660 1069 "q-336133" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311660 1071 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3}+{e}^{t}).$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311660 1072 #uuid "fb32cc38-3d13-5cb4-9fcb-ef6ead5d4613" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311660 1074 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3}+{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{3}+{e}^{t})\\cdot (3{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-2(3{t}^{2}+{e}^{t})\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{3}+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311345 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311661 1069 "q-336138" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311661 1071 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=16\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{4},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=1.$\n\n$v(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311661 1072 #uuid "892d093f-2bcb-5ddf-8b61-53bd37bcc77d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311661 1074 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2\\sqrt{t}}-4{t}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{t}}-4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1}}-4({1}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311341 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311662 1069 "q-336142" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311662 1071 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-24\\sqrt[3]{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=8.$\n\n$v(8)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311662 1072 #uuid "9b98cadb-5783-5aa7-aa5b-ab469955b361" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311662 1074 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-24\\sqrt[3]{t}) \\\\  & =2t-24\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{(\\sqrt[3]{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{8}{{(\\sqrt[3]{t})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=8,$ we substitute $t=8$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(8) & =2(8)-\\frac{8}{{(\\sqrt[3]{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311019 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311663 1069 "q-336146" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311663 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=3{log}_{2}⁡t-2t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to one decimal place.\n\n$v(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311663 1072 #uuid "752c8e69-6c0d-53b3-ae19-5a162922b12b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311663 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{log}_{2}⁡t-2t) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-2 \\\\  & =0.16404… \\\\  & ≈0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311184 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311222 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311664 1069 "q-336164" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311664 1071 "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)={3}^{3t+1}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the position ${s}_{0}$ and velocity ${v}_{0}$ of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n${s}_{0}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}\\,\\text{and}\\,$\n\n${v}_{0}=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311664 1072 #uuid "5f73ef12-4a7e-5b85-befc-d0426bf47ae5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311664 1074 "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)={3}^{3t+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & ={3}^{3(0)+1} \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({3}^{3t+1}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{3t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & ={3}^{3t+2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & ={3}^{3(0)+2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311007 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311420 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311665 1069 "q-336166" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311665 1071 "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)=\\frac{{2}^{t}}{{2}^{t}+1}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the position ${s}_{0}$ and velocity ${v}_{0}$ of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n${s}_{0}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}\\,\\text{and}\\,$\n\n${v}_{0}=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311665 1072 #uuid "9e51aabe-7c8b-5e7f-b26a-fff33d15da87" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311665 1074 "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)=\\frac{{2}^{t}}{{2}^{t}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}}{{2}^{0}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{2}^{t}}{{2}^{t}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({2}^{t}+1)\\cdot {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-{2}^{t}\\cdot {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{t}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{t}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{0}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310911 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311666 1069 "q-336174" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311666 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in feet) at time $t$ minutes is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=1\\,\\text{min}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ft/min}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311666 1072 #uuid "62c296d3-db75-5f2c-8e72-9a9c1243244c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311666 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=1,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-4|=4\\,\\text{ft/min}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311515 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311667 1069 "q-336179" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311667 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-3{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311667 1072 #uuid "b9a79944-17a2-53ad-ab9e-c6b27da56439" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311667 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{6}{2}-6(2) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-9|=9\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311523 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311668 1069 "q-336186" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311668 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in inches) at time $t$ minutes is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=3{t}^{4}-4{e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=0\\,\\text{min}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{in/min}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311668 1072 #uuid "5d5aba3c-6fba-5ab2-884c-39454244c365" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311668 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{4}-4{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{3}-4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=0,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =12\\cdot {0}^{3}-4{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-4|=4\\,\\text{in/min}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311546 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311669 1069 "q-336192" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311669 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your answer to 2 decimal places.\n\nThe speed of the particle is approximately\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311669 1072 #uuid "b9a84fb2-0a5b-573b-b24a-f2106d53fb89" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311669 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}|=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}≈2.47\\,\\text{m/s}$ to 2 decimal places." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311012 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311282 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311368 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1069 "q-336201" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$3x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1072 #uuid "45673dfb-d60d-5849-951f-95ea52c6f106" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311670 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-2,3,1⟩.$  From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-5,2⟩.$  So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,-5,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =⟨-2,3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-19 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-19 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311355 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311671 1069 "q-336213" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311671 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=-2+4t \\\\ z=3+t\\end{aligned}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311671 1072 #uuid "69ff4417-28a7-50ac-aae7-8483ea57f4a8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311671 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,1,0⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,4,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨0,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4 \\\\ 4y+z & =4 \\\\ 4y+z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311070 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311216 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311396 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1069 "q-336220" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1071 "Given the Cartesian equation $3x+y+2z+4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨$ \n{{field-2}}\n$,1\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1072 #uuid "dc5e8637-ca14-5006-a320-1c33620f30f4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311672 1074 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨3,1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 2z & =-3x-y-4 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨3,1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311098 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311342 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311483 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1069 "q-336225" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1071 "Given the Cartesian equation $5x-7y+4z+20=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1072 #uuid "d0599589-1ded-5a0e-8e23-6b2660c198aa" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311673 1074 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,-7,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-7y+4z+20 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position 