duct of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{6.69\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.02}{6.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(2.0237…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(20.237…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.237…\\times {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.237…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(4.498…)\\times {10}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(\\,4.50\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310801 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310832 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311294 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1069 "q-336309" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1072 #uuid "72f5b665-78cb-51a0-bf20-214a07f61466" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311693 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ Line $\\text{L1}$ identifies ${x}_{2}(t)$ as a column of $Φ(t),$ so ${x}_{2}(t)$ must satisfy the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)],$ the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating each column}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310967 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311215 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311694 1069 "q-336314" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311694 1071 "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Venus has a mass of $(4.87\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311694 1072 #uuid "4ad559c9-9cfd-5c87-86a0-09ca886a809d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311694 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (4.87\\times {10}^{24})}{5.54\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 4.87}{5.54}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{24}}{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(11.668…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(1.1668…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.1668…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.1668…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(1.0801…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(\\,1.08\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311280 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311491 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311695 1069 "q-336321" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311695 1071 "Uranus has a mass of $(8.68\\times {10}^{25})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Uranus and Neptune is $(1.63\\times {10}^{12})\\,\\text{m}.$ Accord