x≈-2.8,\\,x≈-0.3.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311244 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311260 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311697 1069 "q-336334" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311697 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3=0,$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311697 1072 #uuid "da6d3a67-8aa8-53c1-8a12-0b5005650a25" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311697 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.6)=36.869\\,897{…}^{∘}≈{36.870}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $36.870$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={36.870}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={36.870}^{∘}.$\nSince $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={36.870}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/38/38b2ec0588022556acd825c6b351d572d52034c6ec5cdeddbdff1dbf7baa11a2.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nX={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{36.870}^{∘}={143.130}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince this value is already inside the required domain, we keep it as our second solution.\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx≈{37}^{∘},\\,x≈{143}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310810 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310993 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311242 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1069 "q-336358" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-8⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,+\\,z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1072 #uuid "ade52e16-a03f-5e87-b722-7d62e66ee938" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311698 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311314 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311699 1069 "q-336371" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311699 1071 "Solve the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Give your answer in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311699 1072 #uuid "70997e85-da8a-563b-a5f3-db7183ab3437" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311699 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{90}^{∘}$ does not lie inside the required domain, we add one full period of ${360}^{∘}$ to find an equivalent angle in the domain:\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThus, our first solution in the required domain is ${x}_{1}={270}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={270}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={270}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311026 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311700 1069 "q-336379" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311700 1071 "Find the exact solution to the equation  $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ Give your answer in radians.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311700 1072 #uuid "e15d0012-e9c7-5d62-85cf-cce438a4c446" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311700 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311300 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311701 1069 "q-336381" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311701 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,1,-1)$, $P(3,4,2)$ and $Q(-1,2,0).$\n\n$\\,y\\,-\\,z\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311701 1072 #uuid "90ebde0b-39f7-53e6-b891-41140a14fc75" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311701 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,p=⟨3,4,2⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,3⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =⟨3,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310812 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311097 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311354 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1069 "q-336384" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-2,1)$, $P(5,0,3)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,11z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1072 #uuid "0b056d61-c09c-50b3-b173-38669f95fc26" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311702 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-2,1⟩,\\,p=⟨5,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,3⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-10i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨-10,4,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =⟨5,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17 \\\\ -10x+4y+11z & =-17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z & =17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z-17 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311276 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311703 1069 "q-336407" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311703 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311703 1072 #uuid "c9d64ce1-7855-51fd-a302-1dae7b7afa6a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311703 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-3,1⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,0,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\nn=⟨4,0,-2⟩,\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =⟨2,-3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute  $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =3 \\\\ 4x-2z & =6 \\\\ 4x-2z-6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311252 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311704 1069 "q-336411" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311704 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+9t.$ Calculate the acceleration $a$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311704 1072 #uuid "4644b039-efe1-51b1-85ca-2213e6cfda66" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311704 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+9t) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-4t+9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311479 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311705 1069 "q-336415" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311705 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{3{t}^{2}-5t+2}{t}.$ Calculate the acceleration $a$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311705 1072 #uuid "16287804-f34d-52ea-a18f-36c3f78a5bd4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311705 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3{t}^{2}-5t+2}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-5+\\frac{2}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-5+2{t}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3+2(-{t}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311054 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311706 1069 "q-336419" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311706 1071 "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+2t.$ Calculate the acceleration $a$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311706 1072 #uuid "1482f25c-c349-5187-b4b3-17d68f17c9a4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311706 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+2t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+1}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311464 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311707 1069 "q-336423" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311707 1071 "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2}+t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at $t=1$ second.\n\n$a(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311707 1072 #uuid "35ec01e2-b742-5c7c-8d11-15d2b5d28c0b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311707 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2}+t) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}-6t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration when $t=1$ second, we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =8(1)^{3}-6(1)+1 \\\\  & =8-6+1 \\\\  & =3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310946 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311708 1069 "q-336429" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311708 1071 "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t+1.$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t>0.$\n\nWhen $a=2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ the velocity is $v=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311708 1072 #uuid "d418f4da-23c3-5896-90ec-594a1accf8cc" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311708 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=2,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3{t}^{2}-6t+2 \\\\ 0 & =3{t}^{2}-6t \\\\ 3t(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $t=2$ since $t>0.$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=2,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}+2(2)+1 \\\\  & =8-12+4+1 \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310802 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311709 1069 "q-336435" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311709 1071 "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+1.$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhen $a=\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ the velocity is $v=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311709 1072 #uuid "7b3aca0f-cf52-5b17-865d-508cddbde631" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311709 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{t+1} \\\\ t+1 & =2 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=1,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)+1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311462 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311710 1069 "q-336441" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311710 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the time when the particle's acceleration is equal to $a=70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe acceleration is $70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at\n$t=$\n{{field-1}} seconds." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311710 1072 #uuid "6a9bb623-416b-50ad-9a1e-54d969b86dcc" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311710 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =24t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=70,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70 & =24t-2 \\\\ 24t & =72 \\\\ t & =3\\text{ }\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is $70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at $t=3$ seconds." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311278 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311711 1069 "q-336444" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311711 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+4\\sqrt{t},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=1$ second.\n\n$a(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311711 1072 #uuid "fe99fc7f-3d36-51f3-b292-37172f0cbe8f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311711 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4{t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2t+2{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =2+2(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1$ second, we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1)^{3}}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311349 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311712 1069 "q-336448" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311712 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=1$ second.\n\n$a(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311712 1072 #uuid "a7eabb9b-6281-59a3-8ec1-45ff039ca063" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311712 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =15{t}^{2}-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =30t-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1$ second, we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =30(1)-4 \\\\  & =26\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311444 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311713 1069 "q-336449" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311713 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}+3t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ second.\n\n$a(\\frac{1}{2})=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311713 1072 #uuid "ac45128c-c263-5b74-919e-49d0aa7b4d11" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311713 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}+3t) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}-2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-2t+3) \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ second, we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{1}{2}) & =24{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311073 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311093 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311274 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1069 "q-336450" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-4,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-3,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+\\,y\\,+$\n{{field-2}} \n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1072 #uuid "b8fc2917-9125-5430-b66d-89b52e1351ff" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311714 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =11i+1j+4k \\\\  & =⟨11,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =⟨2,-4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z-30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311535 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311715 1069 "q-336452" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311715 1071 "The displacement $s$ of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=5+3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311715 1072 #uuid "71d0f7a0-435c-5051-8dc1-239c5e6adc49" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311715 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5+3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =6t-{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311457 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311716 1069 "q-336455" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311716 1071 "The displacement $s$ of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{6}{t+2}.$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311716 1072 #uuid "ff489b9d-3c9a-550f-95cc-a5eab9d34477" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311716 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+\\frac{6}{t+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+6(t+2)^{-1}) \\\\  & =2t-6(t+2)^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-6(t+2)^{-2}) \\\\  & =2+12(t+2)^{-3} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{12}{(t+2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311038 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311717 1069 "q-336459" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311717 1071 "The displacement $s$ of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311717 1072 #uuid "1d397014-aa13-5c53-a91e-67967049b399" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311717 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311114 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311181 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311221 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1069 "q-336481" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the definition of ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ as the standard fundamental matrix by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1072 #uuid "9684981a-5306-5edd-b5f3-7d483bf8c5e8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311718 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution of the system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ By definition, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ satisfies the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nThe second condition here is exactly line $\\text{L4},$ so $\\text{L4}$ follows from the definition of ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ as the standard fundamental matrix by $\\text{using the initial condition }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Substituting ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ from $\\text{L1}$ and $c=x(0)$ from $\\text{L5}$ into $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and substitution from $\\text{L1}\\text{ and }\\text{L5}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311189 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311719 1069 "q-336507" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311719 1071 "Find the exact solution to the equation  $4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Give your answer in radians.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311719 1072 #uuid "1659baa6-3fd4-549c-a4e8-24bfdee6fcd6" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311719 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ does not lie inside the required domain $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ we add one full period of $2\\pi $ to find an equivalent angle in the domain:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThus, our first solution in the required domain is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310891 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311720 1069 "q-336511" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311720 1071 "Find the exact solution to the equation  $2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$ Give your answer in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311720 1072 #uuid "e601be89-d932-5395-8b4c-55d9e3bd61bf" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311720 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310881 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311721 1069 "q-336533" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311721 1071 "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds)." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311721 1072 #uuid "547a5245-a910-5cc0-99d1-bbce9aa1c619" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311721 1074 "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311291 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311722 1069 "q-336534" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311722 1071 "![](images/c6/c644931a5d7c3b015e00518269920718cde39882b45d3589e12e6b90d0391d46.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311722 1072 #uuid "fcff7887-b892-5287-9b6f-79b17b67af6f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311722 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $36\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=36\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=36I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n9=36I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311213 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311723 1069 "q-336538" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311723 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{5}=\\frac{z+2}{-3},\\,y=4\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311723 1072 #uuid "9f9042cb-d3d8-5370-a9db-8978ddd34aee" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311723 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-2,1⟩.$ From the Cartesian equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨5,0,-3⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨5,0,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =⟨3,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =12 \\\\ 5x-3z & =12 \\\\ 5x-3z-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311304 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311724 1069 "q-336540" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311724 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=-2+t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=5\\end{aligned}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311724 1072 #uuid "ebf2e85f-ac82-5cbe-8701-83e9058f130b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311724 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,1⟩.$  From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,4,0⟩.$  So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,4,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =⟨2,-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =-14 \\\\ x+4y & =-14 \\\\ x+4y+14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310834 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311725 1069 "q-336542" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311725 1071 "![](images/91/91c132c39e9dedeb5377d850a344e555ba0755c3fd6183220674cb7f967e943b.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311725 1072 #uuid "aac59797-9ef7-50b5-a749-c3236240b3a7" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311725 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311533 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311726 1069 "q-336543" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311726 1071 "![](images/a7/a7005971c36efa45c4e8c3f752bdc4aec5af3a8b92734e83e85470d10bfbc299.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311726 1072 #uuid "5cbf3848-91d7-5a86-a469-3ae65d829734" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311726 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=16I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=2.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311437 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311727 1069 "q-336546" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311727 1071 "![](images/99/999acaa0bc014b7afc7c916dd03c957e15707ca680836341e5a071a1f321e97d.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311727 1072 #uuid "b05808bc-cc2d-5a25-af93-5fe5382438db" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311727 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=9I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311240 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311728 1069 "q-33655" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311728 1071 "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩$ at the point $P(1,1,4)?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311728 1072 #uuid "e09c97e7-cdca-5648-a0b0-ca6d528dfc46" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311728 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,4)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩ & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ 4t=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, we get $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(1,1,4)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3},\\,2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(1,1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311223 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311729 1069 "q-336590" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311729 1071 "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\nFind, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{80}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311729 1072 #uuid "4200df25-ff4d-51eb-831b-f34b10d9d8a5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311729 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}q=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{40}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{40}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3}+\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{80}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{80},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/3}+\\frac{3}{40} & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25t/3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25(0.05)/3}≈0.034,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311148 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311730 1069 "q-336603" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311730 1071 "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{-7/10}^{-1/5}\\sqrt{10x+7}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311730 1072 #uuid "d1c2d29f-f77c-522c-8fc4-71bcad74829c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311730 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =10x+7 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =10 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=10x+7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -\\frac{7}{10} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ u & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7/10}^{-1/5}\\sqrt{10x+7}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}({5}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}(({5}^{3})^{1/2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311493 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311731 1069 "q-336612" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311731 1071 "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{3}\\cdot {({x}^{4}+1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311731 1072 #uuid "5dc53a57-411e-580b-a40e-954fafb6854b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311731 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{4}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{3}\\cdot {({x}^{4}+1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({2}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311435 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311732 1069 "q-336633" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311732 1071 "You have a stack of seven cards labeled $1$ through $7.$ If one card is randomly drawn, what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311732 1072 #uuid "18ee446d-9856-56cd-b0ee-9f267f88a8bf" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311732 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the label on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S={1,2,3,…,7},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{7}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(2)=…=f(7)=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+…+7\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+…+7(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{2}{7}+…+\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4+5+6+7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{7} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311307 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311733 1069 "q-336640" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311733 1071 "What is the expected value of one roll of a fair tetrahedral die with sides labeled $1,5,7,8,$ respectively?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311733 1072 #uuid "344bd66a-fb8d-5b1c-95de-b6559c935b4e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311733 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the roll of a four-sided die. Then the support is $S={1,5,7,8},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{4}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(5)=f(7)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+5\\cdot f(5)+7\\cdot f(7)+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+5(\\frac{1}{4})+7(\\frac{1}{4})+8(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+5+7+8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4} \\\\  & =5.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310980 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311734 1069 "q-336643" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311734 1071 "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3,\\,I(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311734 1072 #uuid "92ff7b67-920f-5e25-a5d9-2aca35a6388f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311734 1074 "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{1}{2}$ amperes." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311397 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311735 1069 "q-336645" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311735 1071 "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=5,\\,I(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311735 1072 #uuid "2b7d5