0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n${I}_{p}=\\alpha .$ \nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n \n\n$$\n0+25\\alpha =5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-25t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-25t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{5}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310981 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311737 1069 "q-336650" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311737 1071 "![](images/9f/9f527104795b8d264b0d526ccd6c2cd5c2790e4833fa24b169703170715efe12.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311737 1072 #uuid "0c86173b-8393-5a20-9c85-aaf056a98138" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311737 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=24I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+12\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-12(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-12t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-12t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-12t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310977 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311738 1069 "q-336651" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311738 1071 "![](images/62/62dc1d019806908359e3956386f40c1a3ca53d720db7ba0c3122d8eb4ba37c2e.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311738 1072 #uuid "300e9fb1-58c9-585b-b13c-7dc900e6e7db" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311738 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=16I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha =4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A{e}^{-8(0)}+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{-8t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}={e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310870 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311739 1069 "q-336653" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311739 1071 "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $16\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.03$ seconds given that the initial current is $3\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.03$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311739 1072 #uuid "1f82f1e2-e41a-5b2b-b124-a17d0fcfb4a2" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311739 1074 "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, 