ed}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into  $\\text{L1}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310825 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311746 1069 "q-336717" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311746 1071 "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311746 1072 #uuid "999290d3-e880-5065-9964-ee6f1b8bd7a7" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311746 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4x-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 3 \\\\ u & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{(4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{11}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{3}^{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(11)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}ln\\,(\\frac{11}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311154 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311747 1069 "q-336729" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311747 1071 "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{5{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311747 1072 #uuid "cb5be3ea-fe7c-5ca2-a3b9-f4f0c7f3fd0b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311747 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5{x}^{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =20{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =20{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5{x}^{4}+4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{(5{x}^{4}+4)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\text{