e rule $u={x}^{2}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({4}^{5/2}-{1}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(({2}^{2})^{5/2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({2}^{5}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{62}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311264 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311764 1069 "q-336804" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311764 1071 "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{2}}2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311764 1072 #uuid "71d421dc-1ef1-554b-9d91-1ccfe24a307b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311764 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(({3}^{2})^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311061 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311765 1069 "q-336818" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311765 1071 "![](images/1a/1a2cc9b62f3eeed4f2c9559c7f8cc5646872bc43b8076bea92bae50c3c6b29a9.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311765 1072 #uuid "12541a95-3ed9-5a78-aeb0-f1bac7373e1c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311765 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +8\\beta =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{6}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}}≈1.34\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310896 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311766 1069 "q-336847" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311766 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(10,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(10.64<X<18.28).$ Use the tables below and do not round your answer.\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ | $\\,0.9808\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ | $\\,0.9850\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ | $\\,0.9884\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ | $\\,0.9808\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ | $\\,0.9850\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ | $\\,0.9884\\,$ |\n\n$P(10.64<X<18.28)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311766 1072 #uuid "89613f87-fd71-5736-9c29-f319bb743ec0" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311766 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =10$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10.64<X<18.28) & =P(\\frac{10.64-10}{4}<Z<\\frac{18.28-10}{4}) \\\\  & =P(0.16<Z<2.07).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1c1e8b01372821c4ad13bfb1bbfd2a102bb9d247815ace25219d3a3f42c8c2.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =P(Z<2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =Φ(2.07)-Φ(0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.07) & =0.9808 \\\\ Φ(0.16) & =0.5636\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =Φ(2.07)-Φ(0.16) \\\\  & =0.9808-0.5636 \\\\  & =0.4172.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(10.64<X<18.28)=0.4172.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310939 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311767 1069 "q-336874" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311767 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11}{4{x}^{3}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311767 1072 #uuid "29938a59-d760-54c6-a385-71b69e8d780c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311767 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11}{4{x}^{3}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11]^{′}}{[4{x}^{3}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{15{x}^{2}+12x}{12{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1)^{2}+12(1)}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311233 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311768 1069 "q-336879" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311768 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-4x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311768 1072 #uuid "6a9bd8de-23e6-5e4b-8b0f-ac0fc91166b0" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311768 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{2(0)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311025 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311769 1069 "q-336883" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311769 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{x}^{2}+3x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311769 1072 #uuid "9058f7e5-7c1a-537c-ba01-6862fb44012f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311769 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{x}^{2}+3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{2x}-1]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+3x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{2x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2(0)}}{2(0)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{0+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311426 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311770 1069 "q-336891" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311770 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311770 1072 #uuid "227dfde0-1fce-5e67-894b-f76638f73747" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311770 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4\\cdot {x}^{13}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{13}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $4$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13})=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311503 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311771 1069 "q-336894" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311771 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311771 1072 #uuid "49148f92-e2e4-555e-b2fd-c4466e09cc5b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311771 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6\\cdot {x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{7}$ is a power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-6$ changes the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=\\infty . & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7})=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311109 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311772 1069 "q-336897" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311772 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311772 1072 #uuid "2598c90b-1576-53f2-9249-8a4800b3a46b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311772 1074 "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-5)-48}{2x-6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[2{x}^{3}-10x-24]}^{′}}{{[x-3]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-10}{1} \\\\  & =6(3)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311484 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311773 1069 "q-336899" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311773 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311773 1072 #uuid "fe5f1c2f-9b85-5592-be11-466681782951" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311773 1074 "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{′}}{{[({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2x-2}{4x({x}^{2}-9)+32} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{2{x}^{3}-18x+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{2{x}^{3}-18x+16} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[x-1]}^{′}}{{[2{x}^{3}-18x+16]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}-18} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6(1)^{2}-18} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311046 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311774 1069 "q-336901" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311774 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311774 1072 #uuid "045b900e-4eea-59c7-bdae-6b9edf672c1b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311774 1074 "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]}^{′}}{[16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2x-6}{(\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[x-3]}^{′}}{{[4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{-2(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 3}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311500 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311775 1069 "q-336910" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311775 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311775 1072 #uuid "d777916c-9049-5131-a52b-83be981e61c7" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311775 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[2x]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311292 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311776 1069 "q-336931" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311776 1071 "If $p(x)=11{x}^{4}$ and $q(x)=5{x}^{4},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$  \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311776 1072 #uuid "69188dcf-17a4-59f8-bca5-ed5b13a2ac2d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311776 1074 "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11{x}^{4})}{(5{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310871 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311777 1069 "q-336932" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311777 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-$\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311777 1072 #uuid "281dd5df-7f68-5052-a36b-e0c3631c72b1" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311777 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-{9}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311265 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311778 1069 "q-336935" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311778 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311778 1072 #uuid "564f761a-7e2a-5b6b-bf32-97c437e743f7" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311778 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}^{′}}{{[(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311407 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311779 1069 "q-336939" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311779 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311779 1072 #uuid "c6c4283c-1494-5085-aa47-dfd444fd55a1" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311779 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{1+5x}-5)}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[(1+5x)^{-1}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-5(1+5x)^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310883 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311780 1069 "q-336943" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311780 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311780 1072 #uuid "f9ee79b2-bfaf-5068-997a-8ac4b51a6abe" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311780 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311352 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311781 1069 "q-336948" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311781 1071 "If $f(x)=9{x}^{6}$ and $g(x)=-4{x}^{6},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=$  \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311781 1072 #uuid "50835fd0-094c-5563-a823-9e7a69e0be6c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311781 1074 "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{(-4{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310900 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311782 1069 "q-336951" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311782 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311782 1072 #uuid "59aff076-adbf-565d-893c-4346918216f2" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311782 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311057 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311783 1069 "q-336953" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 1