he following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311785 1072 #uuid "b7103803-bbea-5f32-96b2-ff2da6b72265" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311785 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311248 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311786 1069 "q-336967" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311786 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311786 1072 #uuid "dbfa9584-4222-59e9-9ff2-135e4dc942f8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311786 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}}{2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}}{2+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-3/2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/2}}}{2+\\frac{5}{4{x}^{3/2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310952 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311787 1069 "q-336971" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311787 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311787 1072 #uuid "e34adca9-52ea-5918-9113-f6b8e7bf7260" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311787 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310815 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311788 1069 "q-336978" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311788 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}-7{x}^{3}+3x-5)=${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311788 1072 #uuid "b2b181e1-13f1-5696-b7f7-4ce8829e94f7" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311788 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}-7{x}^{3}+3x-5) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310837 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311789 1069 "q-336995" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311789 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}+2x-9)=${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311789 1072 #uuid "52e9f67d-bccf-5eb1-86bd-c4a600645864" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311789 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}+2x-9) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311383 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311790 1069 "q-337003" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311790 1071 "${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(5{t}^{3}+2)}^{4}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311790 1072 #uuid "a7469240-28b0-58b7-b106-4eb003ca4ea9" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311790 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}+2{)}^{4} & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}{)}^{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }625{t}^{12} \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311363 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311791 1069 "q-337032" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311791 1071 "${lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{(4{t}^{3}-t)}^{4}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311791 1072 #uuid "6f0b9a84-632b-576b-a243-42ef8d82f424" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311791 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{(4{t}^{3}-t)}^{4} & ={lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{(4{t}^{3})}^{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }256{t}^{12} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{t}^{12} \\\\  & =256\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311152 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311792 1069 "q-337034" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311792 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{5}-7{x}^{3}+2x-9)=${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311792 1072 #uuid "5d098104-b590-5e0a-abfc-234f5e11d03c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311792 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{5}-7{x}^{3}+2x-9) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311168 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311793 1069 "q-337036" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311793 1071 "The rolls of an unfair six-sided die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311793 1072 #uuid "1e7f2001-555f-5bcb-8305-292988a79232" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311793 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(0.1)+2(0.4)+3(0.15)+4(0.2)+5(0.05)+6(0.1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.8+0.45+0.8+0.25+0.6 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310866 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311794 1069 "q-337039" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311794 1071 "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311794 1072 #uuid "4e1cd3a7-1e89-576a-a200-461092fbc902" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311794 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310992 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311795 1069 "q-337053" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311795 1071 "![](images/2c/2cccb2a1edfeed0a562303053395c6599e14cbf08f9dc5e7f07a4d657755b1ae.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311795 1072 #uuid "17108ac6-2a4e-5a2a-9372-ccc5443d9dd8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311795 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $250\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=250\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=250I=250\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15=250\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311384 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311796 1069 "q-337062" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311796 1071 "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311796 1072 #uuid "7b502da6-93c9-578a-9438-7bf48a08ae11" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311796 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310816 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311797 1069 "q-337063" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311797 1071 "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $80\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.00125\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311797 1072 #uuid "850c8715-57b0-52e9-9bac-08931c47b6d5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311797 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=80\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=80I=80\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.00125}=800q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=80\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+800q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310853 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311006 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311047 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311429 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1069 "q-337081" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1071 "Let $p(x)=9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12,$ and $p(-2)=p(-3)=0.$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $p(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1072 #uuid "c0e86ea4-491c-5d57-a7a2-294b0f8758e3" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311798 1074 "Since $p(-2)=p(-3)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x+2)$ and $(x+3)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x+2)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $9$ | $42$ | $37$ | $-28$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-18$ | $-48$ | $22$ | $12$ |\n|  | $9$ | $24$ | $-11$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12}{x+2}=9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $9$ | $24$ | $-11$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-27$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $9$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6}{x+3}=9{x}^{2}-3x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(x+3)(9{x}^{2}-3x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(x+3)(3x+1)(3x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the four roots. In ascending order, the roots are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3\\,,\\,x=-2\\,,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,,\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311328 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311799 1069 "q-337104" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311799 1071 "![](images/6f/6f9417fdec3a123dbb28ae87a31c6dc49e38327178e1335030b51505f5ca2cbd.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311799 1072 #uuid "df0d9ce1-1303-5e36-b337-412008b9c341" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311799 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $60\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.003\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.003}=\\frac{1000}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310842 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311050 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311461 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1069 "q-337109" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$  Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1072 #uuid "45874040-9e7b-5fb5-9a00-ef344d9b3cd1" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311800 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Starting from $\\text{L3},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe evaluate both sides at $t={t}_{0}$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ $\\text{by substituting }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311543 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311801 1069 "q-337111" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311801 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311801 1072 #uuid "babd6818-2f6d-53fa-bfb6-8440eed031da" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311801 1074 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =\\frac{2}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{15}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{2}{15}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{2}{15}$ coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311417 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311802 1069 "q-337112" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311802 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+7q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311802 1072 #uuid "09b4aa17-88a2-5a0a-9d72-fef341a6e343" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311802 1074 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+7{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+7\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{35}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{35}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-7t}+\\frac{3}{35}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-7t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{3}{35}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{35}$ coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311310 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311803 1069 "q-337113" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311803 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+9q=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311803 1072 #uuid "332813f8-66d0-5a92-805b-c1e46886b0d4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311803 1074 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+9{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+9\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{5}{72}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{72}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}+\\frac{5}{72}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{5}{72}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{5}{72}$ coulombs." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311405 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311804 1069 "q-337114" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311804 1071 "![](images/b9/b91e3582e017882aae67e8b6378074cf941be0c090d9f3f978076583b36c8837.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311804 1072 #uuid "1e2c14cb-00ec-5c0f-ae54-ea9ca961b923" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311804 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{25},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/4}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{125}(6-{e}^{-25t/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{6}{125}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310962 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311084 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311516 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311805 1069 "q-337115" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311805 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a consta