(3,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<13.5).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9505\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9599\\,$ | $\\,0.9608\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9678\\,$ | $\\,0.9686\\,$ | $\\,0.9693\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<13.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311815 1072 #uuid "d7f99ed7-c5c8-5daf-9d23-22c23ed39141" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311815 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =3$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<13.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{13.5-3}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e024a5ac6bdd86c88c6ed6cbe3a647281c63e6934c3830288fa01c1d51997e4a.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.75)=0.9599.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<13.5)=0.9599.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310811 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311816 1069 "q-337183" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311816 1071 "The total number of heads after $4$ flips of an unfair coin follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the total number of heads after $4$ flips of the coin? Do not round the answer.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{2}{21}$ | $\\frac{1}{21}$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311816 1072 #uuid "e9ed81b2-da4e-505d-aa76-858248f5472c" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311816 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{3}{7})+1(\\frac{2}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+3(\\frac{2}{21})+4(\\frac{1}{21}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{6}{21}+\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+6+6+4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310989 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311817 1069 "q-337195" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311817 1071 "A technician reviews inspection logs for defective components. The number of defects found per inspection is summarized below. If $X$ is the number of defective components found in an inspection, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Inspections | $5$ | $7$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Defects | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311817 1072 #uuid "c04aba30-34c3-525b-a65a-fd4f50fe4a8a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311817 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the defects, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of inspections is $5+7+4+3+1=20,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{7}{20} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ P(X=4)=f(4) & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{5}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ | $\\frac{4}{20}$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{5}{20})+1(\\frac{7}{20})+2(\\frac{4}{20})+3(\\frac{3}{20})+4(\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{8}{20}+\\frac{9}{20}+\\frac{4}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310998 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311818 1069 "q-337197" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311818 1071 "A production analyst records the number of flawed units in sampled batches. The data are summarized below. If $X$ is the number of flawed units in a batch, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Batches | $10$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Flawed Units | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311818 1072 #uuid "dea2be0e-fdc0-5db0-86e3-22ebe1a7a0ff" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311818 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the flawed units, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of batches is $10+9+6=25,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{25} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{9}{25} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{10}{25}$ | $\\frac{9}{25}$ | $\\frac{6}{25}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{10}{25})+1(\\frac{9}{25})+2(\\frac{6}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310947 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311819 1069 "q-337198" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311819 1071 "A quality assurance specialist inspects sealed containers for damaged items. The number of damaged items in each container was recorded and summarized below. If $X$ is the number of damaged items in a container, what is the expected value of $X?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| Number of Containers | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Damaged Items | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311819 1072 #uuid "37a09e85-5b87-5925-be0a-eeab91b0e860" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311819 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the damaged items, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of containers is $10+8+6+4+2=30,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{30} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{8}{30} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{6}{30} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{4}{30} \\\\ P(X=4)=f(4) & =\\frac{2}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{10}{30}$ | $\\frac{8}{30}$ | $\\frac{6}{30}$ | $\\frac{4}{30}$ | $\\frac{2}{30}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{10}{30})+1(\\frac{8}{30})+2(\\frac{6}{30})+3(\\frac{4}{30})+4(\\frac{2}{30}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{30}+\\frac{12}{30}+\\frac{12}{30}+\\frac{8}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310948 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311820 1069 "q-337202" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311820 1071 "A stack of cards has $6$ cards labeled with two, $6$ cards labeled with five, and $8$ cards labeled with eleven. If one card is randomly drawn from the stack, what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311820 1072 #uuid "4d888cbf-5ba1-5939-80dc-812c7eab30b1" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311820 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S={2,5,11}.$\n\nIn the stack there are $6$ cards labeled with two, $6$ cards labeled with five, and $8$ cards labeled with eleven (twenty cards in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{6}{20}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(5) & =\\frac{6}{20}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(11) & =\\frac{8}{20}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+5\\cdot f(5)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{10})+5(\\frac{3}{10})+11(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{22}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{44}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+15+44}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311228 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311821 1069 "q-337206" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311821 1071 "A spinner has $1$ section labeled with two, $2$ sections labeled with four, $3$ sections labeled with six, and $4$ sections labeled with eight. If the spinner is spun once,  what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311821 1072 #uuid "c8fea60d-8acd-505a-bf62-74afec768282" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311821 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value shown on a randomly spun spinner. Then the support is $S={2,4,6,8}.$\n\nOn the spinner there are $1$ section labeled with two, $2$ sections labeled with four, $3$ sections labeled with six, and $4$ sections labeled with eight (ten sections in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(4) & =\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(6) & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(8) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+4\\cdot f(4)+6\\cdot f(6)+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{10})+4(\\frac{1}{5})+6(\\frac{3}{10})+8(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{8}{10}+\\frac{18}{10}+\\frac{32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+18+32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{10} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311162 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311822 1069 "q-337207" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311822 1071 "A bag has $4$ tiles labeled with two, $5$ tiles labeled with seven, and $3$ tiles labeled with fifteen. If one tile is randomly drawn from the bag, what is its expected value? Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311822 1072 #uuid "dd95e477-b127-56b9-8e98-54410e77db77" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311822 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn tile. Then the support is $S={2,7,15}.$\n\nIn the bag, there are $5$ tiles labeled with seven, $4$ tiles labeled with two, and $3$ tiles labeled with fifteen (twelve tiles in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(7) & =\\frac{5}{12} \\\\ P(15) & =\\frac{3}{12}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+7\\cdot f(7)+15\\cdot f(15) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3})+7(\\frac{5}{12})+15(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{35}{12}+\\frac{15}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12}+\\frac{35}{12}+\\frac{45}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{8+35+45}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{88}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311200 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311823 1069 "q-337208" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311823 1071 "A jar has $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve. If one chip is randomly drawn from the jar,  what is its expected value? Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311823 1072 #uuid "69a23de5-cd06-5d46-a486-07e0ef32be2a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311823 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn chip. Then the support is $S={1,4,9,12}.$\n\nIn the jar, there are $6$ chips labeled with one, $4$ chips labeled with four, $3$ chips labeled with nine, and $2$ chips labeled with twelve (fifteen chips in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =\\frac{6}{15}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ P(4) & =\\frac{4}{15} \\\\ P(9) & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(12) & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+4\\cdot f(4)+9\\cdot f(9)+12\\cdot f(12) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{5})+4(\\frac{4}{15})+9(\\frac{1}{5})+12(\\frac{2}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15}+\\frac{16}{15}+\\frac{27}{15}+\\frac{24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+16+27+24}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310840 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311824 1069 "q-337223" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311824 1071 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2})$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+1$\n- $y-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}=0$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311824 1072 #uuid "870fa3ac-a172-57d1-b18b-7584f7771a44" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311824 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x({y}^{′})^{2}-{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310906 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311825 1069 "q-337226" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311825 1071 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311825 1072 #uuid "76e64e6f-7a8a-5b8b-bac6-612af4c245fa" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311825 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{2}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311126 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311826 1069 "q-337229" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311826 1071 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311826 1072 #uuid "1b52c57b-852c-58e2-ae93-cc78b2af7920" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311826 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310934 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311111 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311827 1069 "q-337230" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311827 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $2{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311827 1072 #uuid "3c98926c-5044-5e94-be2a-fc638fdc3f93" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311827 1074 "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -8{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311138 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311828 1069 "q-337233" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311828 1071 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}$\n- $y+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311828 1072 #uuid "af31d734-fa0c-53ba-89f5-33969f158e1d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311828 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311331 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311829 1069 "q-337245" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311829 1071 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+({y}^{′})^{2}$\n- $y-x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}+\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}}=0$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311829 1072 #uuid "7a1da43a-e002-579e-9946-31a6ae805b48" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311829 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310804 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310945 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311180 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311243 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311267 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311403 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1069 "q-337259" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1071 "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1072 #uuid "9e2b872a-4e35-5059-b33b-25da543bae2e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311830 1074 "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}({x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})=\\frac{2x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{isn't}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ $\\text{isn't}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311202 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311831 1069 "q-337274" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311831 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311831 1072 #uuid "f72ad52b-3b83-5a1b-b777-f9057dd38453" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311831 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310807 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311832 1069 "q-337276" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311832 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=(2x-7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311832 1072 #uuid "ba49f673-a23b-5512-8ecf-2c4951d1b3ea" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311832 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311085 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311833 1069 "q-337278" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311833 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311833 1072 #uuid "1815ae9e-3063-59f9-a003-5b217f6e9581" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311833 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311027 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311132 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311834 1069 "q-337280" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311834 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}$ |  |  | $\\frac{17}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311834 1072 #uuid "6ac8d1ba-163b-54eb-addd-dba5b1956964" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311834 1074 "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-3{e}^{4t})-3{e}^{4t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311319 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311395 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311835 1069 "q-337281" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311835 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}$ |  |  | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311835 1072 #uuid "a91efb90-d803-5dba-81eb-b92f394b29e5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311835 1074 "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-4{e}^{-3t})-5{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{t} \\\\ -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311058 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311836 1069 "q-337286" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311836 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equati