\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311029 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311841 1069 "q-337334" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311841 1071 "Consider the following game featuring an unfair six-sided die. The probability that the die shows an odd number is $\\frac{2}{3},$ and the probability that the die shows an even number is $\\frac{1}{3}.$ If the roll of a die gives an odd number, the player loses $$4.$ If the roll of a die gives an even number, the player wins $$11.$ What is the expected profit of each roll in this game?\n\n$\\text{E}[p]=$${{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311841 1072 #uuid "026c9bf9-47c1-55e7-813b-b60eba94c35d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311841 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$, $4,$ or $6$), the player wins $$11,$ so we have $p=11.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-4)=\\frac{2}{3},\\,P(11)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-4$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-4)\\cdot f(-4)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =(-4)(\\frac{2}{3})+11(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$1.00.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310835 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311842 1069 "q-337335" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311842 1071 "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2.$ The outcomes of the coin have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{1}{4},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311842 1072 #uuid "01f6d81f-9b7a-55ac-b9f3-3625291ed8bf" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311842 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8,$ so we have $p=8.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $8$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =8\\cdot f(8)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+(-2)(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4}-\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$0.50.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311241 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311843 1069 "q-337336" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311843 1071 "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4.$ The coin outcomes have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311843 1072 #uuid "1d5c7f49-65ed-5617-8670-c9074e9f79de" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311843 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{7}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $5$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =5\\cdot f(5)+(-4)\\cdot f(-4) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3}{10})+(-4)(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{28}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-28}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =-1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.30.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311009 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311844 1069 "q-337337" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311844 1071 "Suppose we play a raffle that costs $$2$ per day. Each player chooses a four-digit number between $0000$ and $9999,$ inclusive, and one winning number is randomly selected each day. The owner of the chosen number each day wins $$2,000.$ What is the expected daily profit of a player in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311844 1072 #uuid "aa822380-c2c7-527f-9d92-45c44d685010" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311844 1074 "Let $p$ represent the daily profit in this game. There are $10,000$ possible numbers that can be selected. One of these numbers is the winning number. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the player's number is selected, they win $$2,000.$ This means the player’s profit is $$2,000-$2=$1,998,$ so we have $p=1998.$\n\n- If the player's number is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(1998)=\\frac{1}{10,000},\\,P(-2)=1-\\frac{1}{10,000}=\\frac{9,999}{10,000}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $1998$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{10,000}$ | $\\frac{9,999}{10,000}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1998\\cdot f(1998)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =1998(\\frac{1}{10,000})-2(\\frac{9,999}{10,000}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1998}{10,000}-\\frac{19,998}{10,000} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18,000}{10,000} \\\\  & =-1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.8.$" 13194139533416 175921860455