ntial equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =1-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =1-\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311511 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311878 1069 "q-337699" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311878 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311878 1072 #uuid "f34ae243-5d21-54e5-a082-840e708d60ba" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311878 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =(1-3t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =(1-3t){e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t-3\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & ={e}^{t}-3(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =4-3t+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+4-3t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=4-3t.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310862 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310968 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311082 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311147 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311273 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311391 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311502 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311510 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [1