stant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=-3,$ and ${a}_{0}=4.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4){x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{3})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+4\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-3\\cdot (3{x}^{2})+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =6x-9{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310879 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311896 1069 "q-337837" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311896 1071 "Given that $D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311896 1072 #uuid "8a1235f2-6c7a-5f14-b04f-a38d354a3d47" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311896 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=-2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311159 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311897 1069 "q-337840" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311897 1071 "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{2}-1?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311897 1072 #uuid "be622853-5ea4-5dac-aa70-1569f1b24b76" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311897 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}={x}^{2},{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+2)({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}-1)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1)+2({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+3(2x)+2({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 2+6x+2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+6x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311259 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311898 1069 "q-337843" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311898 1071 "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-3,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311898 1072 #uuid "46fe989c-8dfc-559c-bcd3-bca514dce992" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311898 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=2x,$ and ${f}_{0}=-3.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-3)\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+2x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-\\frac{3}{x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{3}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311034 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311899 1069 "q-337845" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311899 1071 "Given that $D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}?$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311899 1072 #uuid "c0969102-3c14-5cb9-9e7f-c9144ee9388e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311899 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=4.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3/2}})+3(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3/2}})+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\frac{3}{4{x}^{5/2}}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{3/2}}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{x}^{3/2}}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{3/2}}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311467 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311900 1069 "q-337846" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311900 1071 "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2},$ then $D({x}^{2})=$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311900 1072 #uuid "6eab1339-f915-5dd7-bfe1-352578d84ae4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311900 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=y,{f}_{1}=-2xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=3y.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2})-2x({x}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+3({x}^{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-2x({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x+3{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 2-4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{4} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311062 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311901 1069 "q-337848" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311901 1071 "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y,$ then $D({x}^{2}+1)=$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311901 1072 #uuid "49f57d8f-907e-5009-afba-de523ee59540" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311901 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=y,{f}_{1}=-xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=5.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{2}+1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{2}+1) & =({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+1)-x({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-x({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =7({x}^{2}+1)-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(7-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311520 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311902 1069 "q-337850" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311902 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3)+2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t=(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311902 1072 #uuid "0fdb2e98-21f2-5497-8668-b601a875ae0f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311902 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3}) & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}+C \\\\ x & =-4t-12+C{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x(t-3)+2{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)(t-3)+2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=t-3,\\text{ }g(t)=2{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=2{t}^{2}+t-3.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311329 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311903 1069 "q-337864" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311903 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{4}{t-1}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311903 1072 #uuid "b0da85ea-7176-5967-88c1-f2f71eca61a7" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311903 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{4}{t-1}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{4}{t-1} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =4(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac