8 1071 "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4{y}^{2},$ then $D(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311908 1072 #uuid "354894e0-2841-574a-800b-fa009e2764c8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311908 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=y,{f}_{1}=0,$ and ${f}_{0}=4y.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4{cos}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4{cos}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =-4{cos}^{2}⁡2x+4{cos}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311448 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311489 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1069 "q-337996" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1071 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1072 #uuid "6d738a1f-befb-5d33-811f-bc5d7b9e6626" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311909 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=7,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-{x}^{3},$ which is different from zero." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311466 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311910 1069 "q-338002" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311910 1071 "If $D(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+2y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y,$ then $D({x}^{3}-x)=$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311910 1072 #uuid "81eb695e-b11a-5c13-a61d-21e205d0afa3" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311910 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=x,{f}_{2}=2y,{f}_{1}=0,$ and ${f}_{0}=1.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{3}-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{3}-x) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{3}-x)+2({x}^{3}-x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}-x)+({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{{