examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy-{e}^{x}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ which is different from zero." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310987 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311916 1069 "q-338037" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311916 1071 "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-3n(x+y)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311916 1072 #uuid "1c590dcd-1532-540d-ba51-3f7d0902891d" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311916 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-3n(x+y)} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(x+y)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311115 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311917 1069 "q-338040" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311917 1071 "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-nxy}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311917 1072 #uuid "3c0a6248-969c-53a2-a7ae-a82a71f54bf3" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311917 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-nxy} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{-xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310859 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311918 1069 "q-338050" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311918 1071 "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311918 1072 #uuid "b306e550-2916-514a-a64f-f860060bf5ed" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311918 1074 "Recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=x\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x\\sqrt{2}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x\\sqrt{2}})}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310974 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311534 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311919 1069 "q-338070" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311919 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\nxy\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=xy,\\,Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}-5{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311919 1072 #uuid "2024595f-0932-52b3-a267-c0301403f4b8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311919 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 3/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(yx\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}+5)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-5y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311013 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311379 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311475 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1069 "q-338087" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1071 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+3y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y+9=0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1072 #uuid "e5eca540-4772-55d6-813f-fd6cd2026d5b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311920 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y+9=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y=-9.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-9,$ which is different from zero." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310863 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310905 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311074 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311230 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311387 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311499 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1069 "q-338094" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1071 "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={{field-2}}-2\\cdot {{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1072 #uuid "2d6f9b10-9d35-54d2-b573-00911193c658" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311921 1074 "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2a{y}_{1}-2b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2\\cdot (a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & +ab\\cdot ({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311163 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311922 1069 "q-338102" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311922 1071 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311922 1072 #uuid "d5914278-adab-5478-b262-9e2341a90a4a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311922 1074 "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $7{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311078 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311923 1069 "q-338104" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311923 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311923 1072 #uuid "c1c5937c-0d4d-5848-8380-65f08dc10e61" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311923 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310966 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311924 1069 "q-338142" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311924 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311924 1072 #uuid "291ae9e7-aff4-5123-ac08-1f6fcd3cc238" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311924 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311048 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311925 1069 "q-338186" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311925 1071 "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y,$ then $D({x}^{3}+1)=$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311925 1072 #uuid "54808644-2ac1-5970-a98e-c5094648e233" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311925 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=y,{f}_{1}=-2xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=4.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{3}+1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{3}+1) & =({x}^{3}+1)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{3}+1)-2x({x}^{3}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+1)+4({x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}+1)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(3{x}^{2})-2x({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+4({x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)-6{x}^{3}({x}^{3}+1)+4({x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 6-6{x}^{3}({x}^{3}+1)+4({x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =6({x}^{3}+1)-6{x}^{3}({x}^{3}+1)+4({x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =10+4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{6} \\\\  & =2(5+2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310910 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311926 1069 "q-338188" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311926 1071 "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ then $D({e}^{x})=$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311926 1072 #uuid "30b28e30-4292-5022-95d8-828501bc293e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311926 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=y,{f}_{2}=0,{f}_{1}=-xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={e}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{x}) & ={e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{x})-x({e}^{x})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{x})-x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(1-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311175 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311927 1069 "q-33819" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311927 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 23 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311927 1072 #uuid "8c5d0e74-0671-5cb7-b3b8-23383905b037" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311927 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-3,5,4⟩$ and $w=⟨4,7,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-33i+13j-41k \\\\  & =⟨-33,13,-41⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-33,13,-41⟩.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311348 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311928 1069 "q-33820" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311928 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=⟨3,1,5⟩+t⟨3,-9,-5⟩+s⟨-8,9,3⟩,\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311928 1072 #uuid "6c4d4116-a9f1-5bf2-8a08-5f9441d405d1" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311928 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨3,-9,-5⟩$ and $w=⟨-8,9,3⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -9 & -5 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =18i+31j-45k \\\\  & =⟨18,31,-45⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨18,31,-45⟩.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310872 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311929 1069 "q-33822" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311929 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311929 1072 #uuid "b724a31e-de81-5f97-aa9a-91e913632f49" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311929 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-5,1,1⟩$ and $w=⟨3,1,-2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i-7j-8k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-7,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-3,-7,-8⟩.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311028 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311101 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311930 1069 "q-338230" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311930 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘},$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311930 1072 #uuid "553cafbe-b6f7-5626-916b-7d5745c0a0b8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311930 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/d8/d88379fcad6ee1bfc7f6c2159c814f92ca330dbacee191ebebb027bab306fe2c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={330}^{∘}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310814 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310963 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311931 1069 "q-338252" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311931 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311931 1072 #uuid "7026ce63-415b-5310-a369-96534f7635aa" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311931 1074 "First, we isolate $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/d1/d188a8634c7d33813260472c8691dccdbedbc809d3b59445afc912a43a09675b.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311191 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311932 1069 "q-338254" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311932 1071 "If $D(y)=xy\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3y,$ then $D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311932 1072 #uuid "cefcc57d-275c-5178-8314-12212c2c75ed" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311932 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=xy,{f}_{2}=0,{f}_{1}=0,$ and ${f}_{0}=3.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311257 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311303 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311933 1069 "q-338258" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311933 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘},$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311933 1072 #uuid "4ce3c7f2-3277-5749-9b74-cf88a88b0e03" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311933 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/14/144ed33e7739fbaf17e3f81dd9ae2fd0a2d8e4d622ced7cb5dd60c8d177b6c01.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x={135}^{∘}$ and $x={225}^{∘}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310822 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310965 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311934 1069 "q-338261" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311934 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-