35 1074 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle below the $x$-axis, so the signed area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =(4)(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,8+(-4) \\\\  & =\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311321 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311936 1069 "q-338319" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311936 1071 "![](images/82/82096496503c058ae31473029f7844ddff99fad024dd786c7bad88985279a9af.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311936 1072 #uuid "c469a682-34fe-58c4-a845-14626b1ddbbe" 13194139533416 175921