585 true] [17592186311938 1072 #uuid "c24171a6-04c2-51af-8603-621f0f04a134" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311938 1074 "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-3,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)(2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(6) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =4.5+3+6+9 \\\\  & =22.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310984 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311939 1069 "q-338336" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311939 1071 "![](images/19/19b94b149f752dc284f2cc002d786bc002621333a8eaadf2396e131640046c89.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311939 1072 #uuid "9cd8d854-38ae-5db3-85e9-0ab0d4ba097e" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311939 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,0].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,3].$    The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+0)(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)(2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}-2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311449 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311940 1069 "q-338337" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311940 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311940 1072 #uuid "2031b21a-a65a-54df-8dd6-56c0bb89f676" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311940 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-3{e}^{-3x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =-3-3 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-3x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-3x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}){e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 6{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310848 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311507 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311941 1069 "q-338342" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311941 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{2}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311941 1072 #uuid "72045dd8-ca49-595c-a601-e4817421c93a" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311941 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{7})=1.281\\,044…≈1.2810\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.2810$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=1.3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b1b50c4d13ebae81a4c8af45ec2c9fde8dc6810da78bd21f74ebcdd117a375e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.2810 \\\\  & ≈5.0022 \\\\  & ≈5.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=1.3$ and $x=5.0.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310841 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311361 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311942 1069 "q-338346" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311942 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311942 1072 #uuid "60c9101d-4b05-5101-8e3d-9f6a1a089c95" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311942 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{7})=2.013\\,707…≈2.0137\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.0137$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=2.0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.0137≈1.1279.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.1279.$\n\n![](images/15/157096afa9d3de44710dfa11c99f1f699815156fbcd5d649c1b4048dbbba9913.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.1279 \\\\  & ≈4.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2.0$ and $x=4.3.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310824 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311943 1069 "q-338348" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311943 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311943 1072 #uuid "830bc260-e2f8-5cac-9b8f-36b1fd65e0df" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311943 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x})^{′}-({e}^{4x})^{′}({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})(-4{e}^{-4x})-(4{e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =-4-4 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{4x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-4x})}^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-4x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}){e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 8{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int 2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311288 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311944 1069 "q-338349" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311944 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311944 1072 #uuid "82b12b3d-c4b4-5f3e-9e5f-7a9af2825c06" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311944 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310854 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311945 1069 "q-338355" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311945 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311945 1072 #uuid "e8f18b21-0b34-56a6-ae14-c92176222982" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311945 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{-x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310813 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311946 1069 "q-338378" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311946 1071 "![](images/60/601562e6fab230b818a1776e42b700518f803f425dcb7f407d3659e91db0ff33.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311946 1072 #uuid "e82a73cb-2974-566b-abfe-9071023b39d3" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311946 1074 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3f(x)+2)=3(1)+2=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(f(x)-2)=1-2=-1\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)+2)^{2}}{(f(x)-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{25}{-1}=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310958 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311947 1069 "q-338396" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311947 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311947 1072 #uuid "0a961ddd-0198-531a-af36-6c5853d63ee5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311947 1074 "Applying the root and quotient rules, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3}) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4-8+5 \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{3})}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({x}^{2}-4x+5)}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311419 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311948 1069 "q-338404" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311948 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311948 1072 #uuid "5f4f0442-ec34-5c7c-b112-c31d7b988a07" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311948 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)=3(1)+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule (continuity) in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+2(1)+5}+2(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+2+5}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310923 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311001 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311949 1069 "q-338422" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311949 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311949 1072 #uuid "001033a3-e0b0-54ac-ae25-4f7b5431eaa8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311949 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{4}{9})=2.031\\,350…≈2.031\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.031$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.031≈1.111.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.111.$\n\n![](images/d4/d416faad39ff978c5ad59b91ceee311d358818ee7bf486f0dea95b9ae6319978.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.111 \\\\  & ≈4.253 \\\\  & ≈4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310973 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311950 1069 "q-338426" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311950 1071 "![](images/52/52c11d9ff3afeef74c579d4cb2c5448c9f5b77b7d6569535214bb67408276026.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 131941395