true] [17592186311951 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}){e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311423 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311952 1069 "q-338434" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311952 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311952 1072 #uuid "4a1af344-78c4-54a1-8627-dad2ee177058" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311952 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}){e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 5{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310975 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311953 1069 "q-338474" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311953 1071 "![](images/44/44d4c8752c83db3b64b75b7b115902474856391ea87a21de1466a166816db929.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311953 1072 #uuid "60c910ed-bc89-54a5-8eed-5835bacac1e5" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311953 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the horizontal segment for $x\\in [-4,-1],$ the sloped segment for $x\\in (-1,0],$ and the sloped segment for $x\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle below the $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we subtract its area.\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311103 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311954 1069 "q-338477" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311954 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311954 1072 #uuid "8c7d376c-ad92-5d1f-aa21-41ab3725cef1" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311954 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5(5)}}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{5x})(5+\\sqrt{5x})}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5x})}^{2}}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{25-5x}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5(5-x)}{(5-x)(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5\\cancel{(5-x)}}{\\cancel{(5-x)}(5+\\sqrt{5x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{5(5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5+\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311290 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311955 1069 "q-338481" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311955 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311955 1072 #uuid "4b765cd0-99c3-5b33-a1eb-e67c227ee4a8" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311955 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to }\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & =\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8(8)}}{8-8}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{(8-\\sqrt{8x})(8+\\sqrt{8x})}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{8}^{2}-(\\sqrt{8x})^{2}}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{64-8x}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8(x-8)}{(x-8)(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8\\cancel{(x-8)}}{\\cancel{(x-8)}(8+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{-8}{8+\\sqrt{8x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+\\sqrt{8(8)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8+8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311199 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311956 1069 "q-338484" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311956 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311956 1072 #uuid "b6781a2e-1ec2-5731-b57e-58bd3e75cec3" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311956 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-3-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{({x}^{2}-x-6)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x+1)-4}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+2)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311370 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311957 1069 "q-338490" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311957 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311957 1072 #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311957 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310940 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311958 1069 "q-338496" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311958 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311958 1072 #uuid "2870fd1f-672a-5c18-b50c-82ac76026af2" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311958 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311359 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311959 1069 "q-338499" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311959 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311959 1072 #uuid "f6f2d9f8-7c9e-5660-a01c-0242a16cdd55" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311959 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=64,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16} & =\\frac{64-64}{\\sqrt{4(64)}-16}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16} & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{(\\sqrt{4x}-16)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{(\\sqrt{4x})^{2}-{16}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4x-256} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{(x-64)(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4(x-64)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-64)}(\\sqrt{4x}+16)}{4\\cancel{(x-64)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 64}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x}+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4(64)}+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16+16}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311527 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311960 1069 "q-338504" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311960 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311960 1072 #uuid "9abf0067-9c40-5e64-9990-4f1cf08fbef4" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311960 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & =\\frac{3-3}{3(3)-\\sqrt{27(3)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x-\\sqrt{27x})(3x+\\sqrt{27x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{27x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9{x}^{2}-27x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x(x-3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{27x}}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)+\\sqrt{27(3)}}{9(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310902 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311961 1069 "q-338505" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311961 1071 "![](images/a1/a1a419a984000b3b8559a19809416bcccc9eb8341985994c621c3a48d15109da.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311961 1072 #uuid "24eabe56-3b5d-5bfd-9314-e72f77da398b" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311961 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+\\frac{9}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311247 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311962 1069 "q-338507" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311962 1071 "![](images/8e/8eb7c4fe147028c6dc31aad2427a33bb713553e610101b1fd263bda30f5c49d0.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311962 1072 #uuid "db882e9f-8742-5d10-8490-f9f2d3e0178f" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311962 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-4].$   The corresponding region is a triangle below the $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$   The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0).$   The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,5].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2}+6+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310874 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311963 1069 "q-338511" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311963 1071 "![](images/de/def5dbb4e5a338159e09c098e47e0b78cbe59d31bcd67478f4dbb7b1c85488f9.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311963 1072 #uuid "ffbdf9c1-c61e-5da5-be9b-bb792b3d1ab0" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311963 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the quarter circle for $x\\in [-2,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1).$ The corresponding region is a quarter circle of radius $3,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310808 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311964 1069 "q-338515" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311964 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311964 1072 #uuid "f5ddb91b-80e7-5c82-afbb-8ffde22b8ff2" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true] [17592186311964 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $z=6,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & =\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4} & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2}-4)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(\\sqrt{3z-2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{(3z-2)-16} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3z-18} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{(z-6)(\\sqrt{3z-2}+4)}{3(z-6)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{z\\to 6}\\frac{\\sqrt{3z-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(6)-2}+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-000ffd0e-da3c-5059-86fc-3a45e67d0cf6" 17592186308747 "answer-0013897f-71a9-5870-b05d-26cfd4f50944" 17592186308748 "answer-0027dbd4-8e92-5ef4-b53a-b2ea5dc8b3f0" 17592186308749 "answer-0057fe33-994d-530f-9d6a-1d8d52f3e80d" 17592186308750 "answer-0059f48a-5ee1-5b39-ad9d-4e15ebe01384" 17592186308751 "answer-007369ab-f779-5cec-ab3a-4cd8748045f6" 17592186308752 "answer-0087b403-c7ca-5331-b8d9-439842b47d07" 17592186308753 "answer-00c6d2d4-b509-561c-8367-3eaf56800a0a" 17592186308754 "answer-00e5e6c5-ab44-55be-aa90-70e3ce45de4c" 17592186308755 "answer-00e8ad51-9c9e-58fe-a872-6f16f89b14f6" 17592186308756 "answer-013fe3e7-48a3-5036-84c8-ff7674a40c54" 17592186308757 "answer-015babbe-537b-546c-9be6-443bb2d4bc2c" 17592186308758 "answer-01633495-e9fe-50bd-a637-b4862253307f" 17592186308759 "answer-016363b1-eb00-5b79-8366-af2b32a79bd9" 1759218630876